{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 as the only son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability as his father was assassinated when he was still a child. Raymond III inherited the title at the age of twelve but could not rule independently due to his youth. A regency was established under the guidance of his mother and later under the supervision of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. His minority lasted nearly a decade and during this time Tripoli remained a vassal state under the influence of the more powerful Kingdom of Jerusalem. His upbringing was shaped by the constant threat of Muslim forces and the need to maintain Christian control over the Levant.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III came of age in 1156 and began to assert his authority as Count. He quickly became a key player in the politics of the Crusader states. He was known for his cautious diplomacy and his desire to avoid unnecessary conflict. He often sought alliances with Muslim rulers to ensure the stability of his territory. His relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem was complex. He sometimes clashed with the royal family especially with Queen Melisende and later with her son Baldwin III. Raymond was also a member of the High Court of Jerusalem and played a major role in the succession disputes that followed the death of Baldwin III. He supported the claim of Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and was seen as unfit to rule by many nobles. Raymond’s influence grew as he became regent for Baldwin IV in 1174.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III’s final years were dominated by the rise of Saladin and the collapse of Christian unity in the Holy Land. He was present at the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader army was crushed. He survived the battle but was captured by Saladin. He was held prisoner for several months before being released. His health deteriorated rapidly after his release and he died later that year. His death marked the end of an era of cautious diplomacy in the County of Tripoli. He was succeeded by his son Raymond IV. Raymond III is remembered as a pragmatic leader who tried to preserve his lands through negotiation rather than confrontation. His legacy is one of quiet resilience amid the chaos of the Crusades. He was not a flamboyant warrior but a steady administrator who understood the fragility of Christian rule in the East.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.0057143899612128735}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.008746527135372162}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09061263501644135}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -9.65590606938349e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.000750736624468118}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00020358874462544918}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -4.970903682988137e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.31327664852142334}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0015511397505179048}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e